     "text": "**What happens if $h_2(t) = \\delta(t)$?**\n- If $h_2(t) = \\delta(t)$ (an ideal identity/wire system), the injected disturbance convolved with $h_2(t)$ is:\n  $$b\\,\\delta(t) * \\delta(t) = b\\,\\delta(t)$$\n- At the subtractor:\n  $$b\\,\\delta(t) - b\\,\\delta(t) = 0$$\n- The disturbance cancels completely, leaving:\n  $$y(t) = x(t) * h_1(t) * \\delta(t) = x(t) * h_1(t)$$\n- In this degenerate case, the system becomes **both Linear and Time-Invariant (LTI)**!\n- The condition $h_2(t) \\neq \\delta(t)$ was added specifically to prevent this cancellation, illustrating how internal disturbances propagate through dynamic stages to break external system properties."
